A||||9||Phantom Rage||R134a Service port adapter question||||5||
Z||000000||Phantom Rage||07-08-2001||11:48 PM||||(for 88 coupe)<P>My accumulator has two ports on it.  From what I understand this is where the low side R134a adapter service port goes.  Which port should I put the low side R134a adapter on, the one closer to the passenger side or the one closer to the driver side?<P>Do I have to remove the accumulators port valve?  It seems to be the only way the adapter will go on.<P>My Napa R134a conversion kit came with three port fittings.  Only two of the fittings seem to fit, is the third one just an extra perhaps for some other GM vehicle?  <P>Of the two included high side port adapters only the 90 degree swiveled (L) shaped fitting seems to fit.  The part that swivels seems to leak air when blown into.  Will it seal up when tightened down?  <P>Also the high side adapter does not seem to fit unless the port valve is removed.  Does the valve have to be removed as well?  If so with what, the valve seems smaller than the typical valve and my bike tube valve remover does not seem to fit?   <P><BR>Sorry for all the questions!  <BR>As always thank you, for any reply <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Leadfoot||07-09-2001||01:46 PM||||Good questions.  I am doing a r134 conversion myself and I am also a bit confused to where the ports go.  Does someone have the 411?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||07-09-2001||03:27 PM||||I just did a 134 conversion on my 88.  It sounds like you are using the same type of kit I did.  If so, then the valve cores will have to be removed before installing the service ports. On the accumulator the service port goes on the passenger side fitting.  The pressure switch goes on the driver's side.  The high side fitting is the 90 degree one.  I had no leaks even though it would still swivel after it was tightened down.  I don't know what the extra fitting goes on, but it's not a Fiero.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000003||88CoupeV6||07-09-2001||05:30 PM||||Here is where I hooked them up on my 88. Low side is the blue fitting and red is the high side.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ac.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by 88CoupeV6 (edited 07-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Leadfoot||07-09-2001||06:02 PM||||Thanks 88CoupeV6 that is just what I needed! <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Johnny07||07-09-2001||09:37 PM||||I just did the same conversion on Saturday. All the info is good the only thing I can add is that the extra fitting is included "for some GM cars '92 and up" so don't worry about it. BTW how much was the kit. I got 2 from Kmart for $30 each but have only used 1. If it's cheaper I'll return it cause I actually need 3 more!||||reg||1
Z||000006||88CoupeV6||07-10-2001||10:22 AM||||$35 at the local wally world(Wal-Mart) around here||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phantom Rage||07-10-2001||02:49 PM||||Wow thanks for the great info.  I don’t know why but my accumulator seems to be missing the pressure switch.  All I know is that it worked before, so maybe for some strange reason GM or the previous owner had it moved elsewhere.<BR> 	I got my kit from Napa, since I could not find the Castrol Kit through the local part store chains.  The kit was surprisingly cheap for a Napa product, only $27.  The kit came with 8oz-ester oil, O-rings, port adapter fittings, R134a retrofit sticker, and instructions.<BR>	I still can’t get that valve out of the smaller high-pressure port.  I hope the local bike shop has a small enough valve tool.  	<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">Thanks again!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">    <BR>||||reg||7
